1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,963
Two men in a canoe, dying of thirst, study a stolen map.

2
00:00:04,963 --> 00:00:07,515
Here is the reef, with a gap.

3
00:00:07,515 --> 00:00:09,129
Here is the river.

4
00:00:09,129 --> 00:00:14,214
A dotted line runs from the gap to a clump of palms, and a star marks the place.

5
00:00:14,433 --> 00:00:19,851
And scattered round the star, some little dashes, pointing this way and that.

6
00:00:19,851 --> 00:00:21,804
Neither man can explain them.

7
00:00:21,804 --> 00:00:24,304
They will find out what they mean, one minute too late.

8
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:36,334
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 4: The Treasure in the Forest, by H. G. Wells.

9
00:00:38,733 --> 00:00:39,854
Six parts.

10
00:00:39,854 --> 00:00:41,235
H. G. Wells.

11
00:00:41,235 --> 00:00:42,901
The map and the dream.

12
00:00:42,901 --> 00:00:44,229
Into the forest.

13
00:00:44,229 --> 00:00:45,558
The trap explained.

14
00:00:45,558 --> 00:00:47,875
The characters and themes.

15
00:00:47,875 --> 00:00:50,815
And how Wells builds suspense, clue by clue.

16
00:00:55,167 --> 00:01:00,611
Herbert George Wells was born in eighteen sixty six to a poor family in Kent.

17
00:01:00,611 --> 00:01:09,908
Apprenticed as a draper at thirteen, he hated it, won a scholarship, and studied biology under Thomas Huxley, Darwin's great defender.

18
00:01:09,908 --> 00:01:15,325
This story appeared in a London magazine in eighteen ninety four, when he was twenty seven.

19
00:01:15,325 --> 00:01:23,355
Within four years he had written The Time Machine, The Invisible Man and The War of the Worlds, and became a father of science fiction.

20
00:01:23,567 --> 00:01:26,108
But this story is not science fiction.

21
00:01:26,108 --> 00:01:33,530
It is a tight adventure and mystery with a sting in the tail: a lesson in how to plant a secret in plain sight.

22
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:43,145
Evans dozes in the canoe, and his dream tells us the back story.

23
00:01:43,145 --> 00:01:48,171
Long ago a Spanish galleon ran aground and its gold was buried.

24
00:01:48,171 --> 00:01:57,898
Two hundred years later a Chinese man, Chang-hi, found the gold and reburied it single handed, laying great stress on how safe it was.

25
00:01:57,898 --> 00:02:01,401
Evans and Hooker overheard him by a campfire.

26
00:02:01,401 --> 00:02:07,117
Then the dream jumps to Chang-hi's pigtail in Evans's hand: they took the map by force.

27
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:10,993
Notice: the story never says they killed Chang-hi.

28
00:02:10,993 --> 00:02:12,348
It only implies it.

29
00:02:12,348 --> 00:02:24,040
And in the dream Chang-hi gives a startling, incomprehensible grin, and the gold becomes a furnace, with a devil who looks like him feeding Evans burning coals.

30
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:25,501
Two warnings, already.

31
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:34,247
They land, drink from the river, and walk into the jungle.

32
00:02:34,247 --> 00:02:37,359
Wells builds the setting as a contrast.

33
00:02:37,359 --> 00:02:41,786
The beach is blazing glare, dry lips, thirst and hurry.

34
00:02:41,786 --> 00:02:50,705
The forest is cool shadow under trees like vast pillars, so silent that Evans shivers: it seems almost cold here.

35
00:02:50,705 --> 00:02:55,388
From bright and open to dark and enclosed: from hope to danger.

36
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:05,925
By the stream they find a dead Chinese man, face down, in blue, beside a spade, a heap of stones and a freshly dug hole.

37
00:03:05,925 --> 00:03:07,565
Somebody has been here before.

38
00:03:07,565 --> 00:03:16,536
Evans swears and stamps; Hooker turns white and notices the dead man's neck is puffed and purple, his hands swollen.

39
00:03:16,536 --> 00:03:19,688
Then Hooker looks into the hole: You fool, it's all right.

40
00:03:19,688 --> 00:03:20,898
It's here still.

41
00:03:20,898 --> 00:03:22,325
Dull yellow bars of gold.

42
00:03:23,533 --> 00:03:25,032
Now watch the thorns.

43
00:03:25,032 --> 00:03:30,748
Evans climbs into the hole and heaves out a heavy bar; a little thorn pricks his hand.

44
00:03:30,748 --> 00:03:35,175
He pulls it out and carries on, piling ingots on his jacket.

45
00:03:35,175 --> 00:03:36,673
Another little thorn.

46
00:03:36,673 --> 00:03:42,545
He grows irritable, his arms ache, the forest feels stuffy, he snaps at Hooker.

47
00:03:42,545 --> 00:03:46,777
Then he stumbles, clutches his throat, and slides down a tree.

48
00:03:46,777 --> 00:03:52,004
And as Hooker gathers up the gold, he feels a little prick on the ball of his thumb.

49
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:59,393
At that moment Hooker puts it all together.

50
00:03:59,393 --> 00:04:07,713
He looks at Evans twitching on the ground, at the blue clad body through the trees, and he thinks of the little dashes on the map.

51
00:04:07,713 --> 00:04:09,653
In a moment he understands.

52
00:04:09,653 --> 00:04:17,973
The dashes marked poisoned thorns, like those the Dyaks of Borneo use in blowpipes, planted all round the treasure.

53
00:04:17,973 --> 00:04:20,747
That is what Chang-hi meant by very safe.

54
00:04:20,747 --> 00:04:22,252
And he understands that grin now.

55
00:04:23,467 --> 00:04:31,164
Hooker sucks the wound for dear life, but the ache creeps into his arms and throat, and he knows it is no good.

56
00:04:31,164 --> 00:04:35,760
He sits by the pile of gold and watches his companion's body quiver.

57
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:43,065
A breeze stirs high above, and the white petals of some unknown flower come floating down through the gloom.

58
00:04:43,065 --> 00:04:44,315
Wells stops there.

59
00:04:44,315 --> 00:04:46,424
He does not tell us Hooker dies.

60
00:04:46,424 --> 00:04:47,394
He does not need to.

61
00:04:52,167 --> 00:04:53,588
Three characters.

62
00:04:53,588 --> 00:04:59,929
Evans: hot tempered and greedy, swearing at the corpse and digging on after two pricks.

63
00:04:59,929 --> 00:05:06,583
Hooker: careful and watchful, the story's eyes, the one who understands, too late.

64
00:05:06,583 --> 00:05:15,268
And Chang-hi, who never appears on stage, yet wins: the shrewd man the robbers looked down on out-thinks them from beyond death.

65
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:17,426
Themes.

66
00:05:17,426 --> 00:05:26,515
Greed and its punishment: every stage of greed leads them deeper into the trap, and the moral is acted out, never stated.

67
00:05:26,515 --> 00:05:33,455
Appearance and reality: nothing is what it seems, and each false appearance costs a life.

68
00:05:33,455 --> 00:05:43,897
Racism and its blindness: they think a Chinese life is scarcely sacred, and that contempt stops them imagining Chang-hi could be cleverer than they are.

69
00:05:43,897 --> 00:05:47,223
And rough justice: the man they wronged defeats them.

70
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,127
The story is a lesson in foreshadowing.

71
00:05:55,127 --> 00:05:58,226
Every detail is a clue planted early.

72
00:05:58,226 --> 00:06:01,077
The little dashes on the map: the thorns.

73
00:06:01,077 --> 00:06:04,619
Chang-hi's stress on safety: a booby trap.

74
00:06:04,619 --> 00:06:06,533
His grin: he knew.

75
00:06:06,533 --> 00:06:09,814
The nightmare of burning coals: punishment.

76
00:06:09,814 --> 00:06:13,421
Even the forest feeling almost cold: danger.

77
00:06:13,421 --> 00:06:16,666
Read it twice and every line points to the ending.

78
00:06:17,867 --> 00:06:19,561
Other devices to quote.

79
00:06:19,561 --> 00:06:24,093
Dramatic irony: we guess about the thorns before Evans does.

80
00:06:24,093 --> 00:06:28,897
Situational irony: they die of the very thing that protects the treasure.

81
00:06:28,897 --> 00:06:30,785
Suspense around the corpse.

82
00:06:30,785 --> 00:06:34,913
Pathetic fallacy: the silent forest seems to watch.

83
00:06:34,913 --> 00:06:37,608
Understatement: somebody has been here before.

84
00:06:37,608 --> 00:06:40,389
And an open ending that the reader completes.

85
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:43,570
Pause and think.

86
00:06:50,867 --> 00:06:56,182
Poisoned thorns set round the gold: that is why Chang-hi said it was "very safe".

87
00:06:58,167 --> 00:06:58,602
One more.

88
00:07:06,233 --> 00:07:09,640
Chang-hi knew the robbers would walk into his poisoned thorns.

89
00:07:11,833 --> 00:07:12,889
Quick recap.

90
00:07:12,889 --> 00:07:16,001
Wells, and a tale from eighteen ninety four.

91
00:07:16,001 --> 00:07:18,423
A stolen map and a guilty dream.

92
00:07:18,423 --> 00:07:25,259
From the blazing beach to the silent forest, a corpse, gold, and three little pricks.

93
00:07:25,259 --> 00:07:27,603
The dashes were poisoned thorns.

94
00:07:27,603 --> 00:07:32,594
Greed punished, appearances that lie, and contempt that blinds.

95
00:07:36,767 --> 00:07:38,813
That was The Treasure in the Forest.

96
00:07:38,813 --> 00:07:46,886
Next, a homecoming in China, and a childhood friend who now calls the narrator master: My Old Home, by Lu Xun.

97
00:07:46,886 --> 00:07:47,451
See you there.

